\subsection{Human Data Validation}
\label{sec:human_data}

A key concern is whether the evidence hierarchy reflects genuine discourse-level generalization or merely within-LLM stylistic transfer, since both training and OOD evaluation data are generated by Qwen3-8B. We test this by evaluating on ToxicChat~\citep{lin2023toxicchat}, a dataset of 204 human-written jailbreak attempts and 204 benign conversations collected from real LMSYS user interactions, entirely independent of any LLM generation pipeline.

All domain-adapted variants substantially outperform vanilla on human data (F1): JB-MLM (0.842$\pm$0.009) $>$ TF-IDF (0.710) $>$ scrambled (0.667$\pm$0.030) $\approx$ 9-class (0.654$\pm$0.087) $\gg$ vanilla (0.221$\pm$0.055). The 3$\times$+ gap between any domain-adapted variant and vanilla refutes the hypothesis that the evidence hierarchy is a Qwen3-8B generation artifact: DAPT on synthetic jailbreak text transfers to human-written attacks.

JB-MLM outperforms 9-class by 18.8\,pp on human data, reversing the ranking observed on synthetic OOD evaluations (where 9-class $\geq$ MLM). ToxicChat contains single-turn jailbreaks with no multi-turn escalation structure, so the classification head's regularization of turn-sequence representations provides no advantage. MLM's unsupervised domain exposure, which captures token-level distributional properties without imposing synthetic-data-specific classification boundaries, transfers more robustly across the human/LLM style gap. This reversal strengthens the regularizer interpretation from Section~\ref{sec:decomposition}: classification supervision shapes representations for the training distribution's sequential structure, not for universal jailbreak detection.

%----------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Perturbation Analysis}
\label{sec:perturbation}

\paragraph{Single-LLM confound.}
Both training and synthetic OOD data are generated by Qwen3-8B, raising the concern that the evidence hierarchy reflects within-LLM stylistic transfer rather than discourse-level generalization. Evaluation on ToxicChat~\citep{lin2023toxicchat} (204 human-written jailbreaks from LMSYS) provides partial resolution: all domain-adapted variants outperform vanilla by 3$\times$+ (Section~\ref{sec:human_data}). JB-MLM (0.842) outperforms 9-class (0.654) on this single-turn human data, reversing the synthetic-data ranking. This reversal is consistent with the regularizer interpretation: classification supervision optimizes for the training distribution's multi-turn sequential structure, while unsupervised MLM captures token-level distributional properties that transfer more broadly across the human/LLM style gap. The single-turn nature of ToxicChat means this evidence does not validate the full multi-turn pipeline; multi-turn human jailbreak datasets are needed to confirm the complete hierarchy.

\paragraph{Encoder capacity confound.}
The cross-encoder comparison uses DeBERTa-v3-base (184M parameters) and RoBERTa-base (125M parameters), which differ in both pretraining objective and model capacity. We cannot fully disentangle whether DeBERTa-v3's greater DAPT needs reflect its RTD pretraining or its larger capacity. Size-matched comparisons (e.g., DeBERTa-v3-base vs.\ RoBERTa-large) would isolate the pretraining objective effect and are left for future work.

